From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Date: Tue, 12 Apr 1994 15:04:11 -0400
Subject: [#94-4-78] IIA: Free Internet Accounts.

  I sent an application account to International Internet Association a couple
of months ago, together with my credit card number, and felt nervous for a few
weeks after that...
  Now I have an account there, and they seem to be doing a good job:
the system is running well, they have a sufficiently large set of services
and utilities that you can access from Unix shell if you know what you are
doing and from a convenient menu if not.
   
   There are two problems that make the system unusable for me at this point:
long-distance access and small disk space limit (1 to 2 megs).
But things may change...

   I am somewhat skeptical about the whole idea though.
Providing free net access to everybody who wants it?
  First, I am not sure it *should* be done: taking money from people who think
net access is worth it and giving it to those who doubt it?
Probably, the flow of finances in the *opposite* direction would make more
sense...
 Second, I'm not sure it *can* be done: if we want to provide free access
to at least tens of millions of people nationwide, we'll have to spend
billions of dollars, keep tens of thousands of people employed supporting
the whole thing, etc.  Private donations and volunteers will hardly provide
that.

  I would expect that IIA would be choked with account requests, and will
start either imposing severe usage restrictions or charging for the system
use.

    chislenk@iia.org

