Subject: [#94-4-79] Land vs Other Property

Dan Davis says-

> Steve asks many "why shoulds" with respect to property....
> 
> In both of these cases, should is being used in the sense of
> obligation. Since I am not big on moral duty, if you believe in such
> things, you'll have to get an answer from someone else. 

By asking "Why should," I hoped to get both specific answers and "meta"
answers like your statement above (preferably the former justified in
terms of the latter, actually).  "Should" is a shorthand that people
use in different but related ways; I wanted people to reveal theirs.

> > If you think there should be some sort of equality between your/our 
> > wishes and other people's, what the heck for?  Does it have something to
> > do with workability or getting other people to agree to it?  If so, aren't
> > you bowing down before their ability to coerce you?
> 
> Dealing with people in such a way that you take into account the power
> they have to harm you strikes me as quite rational. If you consider
> that "bowing down", make the most of it. 

Someone (thought it was you, sorry) had said that respecting land property 
because of the occupant's gun wasn't a good enough reason.  I'm asking, why 
not, and why especially for land?

> > Why do you have a right to an opinion on the matter?
> 
> I never said I had any such right. I don't check for rights before I
> form opinions.

I'm asking, (to others if not you), when you think of different kinds of
social arrangements as better and worse, is there some point to your having
such an opinion?  Is it just blue sky?  Just useful for choosing where to
move?  Or do you think that your ideas might effect others?  If the latter,
then why?  Why might others care what you think?  The reason I ask is
that I think it illuminates the real reasons for property, to think about 
myself and people I might interact with, instead of thinking more
abstractly.

-fnerd
