From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Tue, 12 Apr 94 16:44:46 EDT
Subject: [#94-4-81] land property

Dan Davis says-
> 
> Property in land has little to do with clods of dirt, and much more to
> do with marking of surface territory over the planet, of which there
> is not so much unclaimed to go around.

Yes, it's not exactly clods of dirt.  But none of us seems to have the
energy to go into exactly how it differs and why the differences might
matter.  (Food differs from clothing too, but Dan is not suggesting that
this is such a big categorical difference.)  Also none of us seems to
have the energy for a complete essay on property rights.  I'll offer
a short paragraph, though.  But first I want to quip: there's *even
less* unclaimed food, clothing, stereo equipment...than unclaimed
surface territory.  So once again, why should surface territory be
the special case?

Here's my short theory of property.  Property is what you control 
access to.  That needs to take into account the social and mutual-
support systems that exist around access control.  Sometimes there
are situations where someone has control now, but various people 
are hoping go take control from that person and give it to another.
In that case the "hopeful" people are thinking that someone has
effective control in the long term even if not in the short term.

All of that is general enough that it applies equally to land and 
other things.  It even applies to "intellectual property," which I
have problems with.  

-fnerd
