Subject: [#94-4-86] RAPTOR: A trojan horse of anarchocapitalism ?

1. Where to get the game:

    Shareware version of Raptor is now available at the following FTP sites:


        ftp.uml.edu             /msdos/Games/Apogee
        wuarchive.wustl.edu     /pub/MSDOS_UPLOADS/games/Apogee

   ( FILENAME: 1raptor.zip )

   You can also find it on Software Creations BBS and many other BBSs.

   You can also order the full version from Apogee for ~$35 + S/H.

2.  SimAnarchy, etc.

    I think it still should be called SimCity, just as the current CimCity
is called by what it is, and not by how it is run.
The multiplayer game should allow different organizational setups, so the
choice can be left up to a GameOp.   In the very same world, different people
may want to play SimSchool, SimCasino, SimBrothel, SimKFC, SimUnderground,
SimArtGallery, etc.  Other Sim's can provide a player with resources,
challenges and entertainment, so it makes sense to have them all accessible
in one world.
 
   Then the players can decide  whether they want  to open their SimArtGallery
under socialism or anarchy, and if Statists can make their efforts in creating
new game entities [SimRealEstateDevelopment] more profitable under rent control
than the anarchist GameOps would do without it.


3.  Games with changing rules.

   All [most?] existing games are essensially statist in the sense that they
impose a fixed set on rules that the players don't get to change.
 
 I've been contemplating a game where players could use their turns to change
not only the position according to the rules (e.g., move a pawn),  but change
the rules themselves [according to some meta-rules] - e.g., declare that from
now on a player can use their turn to: swap two men on the board, move a pawn
forward for taking a shot of vodka,  try to shoot the partner's  men  with  a
gun from no closer than 30 feet,etc. You can also change meta-rules according
to meta-meta-rules  [e.g.: you can't suggest rules that would cost body parts
to your partner's family members, etc.]
   In my experience, people are usually uncomfortable with this idea, since
they are more inclined to seek simple pleasures in something familiar, and
develop skills in some standard area that they can show off to other folks.
   With changing rules they lose this simple comfort, and have to think for
themselves and be creative.
   Most people feel that this is too complex and without somebody telling
them the goal of the game they lose the sense of direction.
   Just like in the Real World they live in...

   Still, a Game-with-Changing-Rules would much closer imitate the Real World,
and would attract the most interesting players.
 
