> 
> ...  But none of us seems to have the
> energy to go into exactly how it differs and why the differences might
> matter.  ...  Also none of us seems to
> have the energy for a complete essay on property rights.  

Ha ha, well yes, you noticed that too. It is not entirely just lack of
energy on my part, however. Look at my position. You claim that those
without land should respect the claims of the landed. If I come up
with a theory on property rights, and show how it is not sufficient
for your claim, you'll just say my theory is wrong, or at least
incomplete. 

The bottom line place where I hope we can agree is that we should
avoid using force against each other; that is it undesirable for us to
get into situations in which we come into physical conflict.  You have
proposed a solution in which some people have an advantage in the
situation, and claim a right to use physical force against others,
while I have proposed one roughly symmetric. The all other things
being equal baseline would be a symmetric relationship, so I think you
have the burden of proof.

> All of that is general enough that it applies equally to land and 
> other things.  It even applies to "intellectual property," which I
> have problems with.  

Property as an institution of mutually enforced control makes for a
useful concept to start with. You've given a description which suits
me well enough. How about the prescription? When is it mutually
advantageous, and roughly the best that either of us can expect to do
in the negotiations, for us to agree with the other guy that he gets
a certain control?

